1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,107
How does our system talk to Uzcard and Humo, when they speak different languages?

2
00:00:05,107 --> 00:00:11,369
Uzcard speaks JSON-RPC, and Humo speaks SOAP, with different fields and different errors.

3
00:00:11,369 --> 00:00:18,880
If the payment code knew both, every new scheme would mean touching payment code, and no other project could reuse the work.

4
00:00:18,880 --> 00:00:27,380
So each scheme gets its own adapter: a separate Spring Boot service with its own OpenAPI contract, that any service or project may call.

5
00:00:27,380 --> 00:00:34,678
Every adapter answers plain REST with one envelope: a request id, the scheme's decision, and the data.

6
00:00:34,678 --> 00:00:41,440
Inside, every scheme method is one command, picked by its type from a factory, the same way in both adapters.

7
00:00:41,440 --> 00:00:47,619
What a scheme's codes mean lives in a status file: which codes approve, and which declines they stand for.

8
00:00:47,619 --> 00:00:58,761
Uzcard debits in one step, by the card's token, with our payment id as the external reference; if that reference was sent before, the adapter looks the payment up instead of paying twice.

9
00:00:58,761 --> 00:01:03,226
Humo holds the money first, and the card module captures the hold right away.

10
00:01:03,226 --> 00:01:10,202
The card module needs only the published client library and one port, a card processor, with one processor per scheme.

11
00:01:10,202 --> 00:01:16,047
Every answer becomes one of three outcomes: approved, declined with our own code, or unknown.

12
00:01:16,047 --> 00:01:24,130
A timeout answers five oh four, and the card module reads it as unknown, never a decline, because the money may already have moved.

13
00:01:24,130 --> 00:01:28,130
Next, knowing a card's scheme, and storing its number safely.

